o none, so pass prefix whenever the documentation shows a word before the key, Bearer included. The form cannot save a key with any other word before it: for an API that expects one, the user adds the key in the environment's settings. Not used with suggested_type \"basic\". Never put a key's value in this or any other field.", "type": "string"}, "reason": {"description": "One or two sentences: which request failed or which step needs the credential, and what it unblocks. The form may not show this, so say in your message, before you call, which network secret you need, for which host, and why. The secret's value is entered only in the form, never in chat. Plain text only: no URLs, links or markup (name the host instead).", "type": "string"}, "suggested_type": {"description": "Optional hint for the form's default: \"bearer\" for a token or API key sent in a header (Authorization: Bearer unless header_name and prefix say otherwise), \"basic\" for username and password.", "enum": ["bearer", "basic"], "type": "string"}}, "required": ["host", "reason"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_credential"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Schedule a message to be delivered back into THIS SESSION at a future time. Use it to remind yourself to resume work, check on something, or continue after a delay. If a turn is running in this session when the message is due, the message waits and is normally delivered after that turn ends. It does not interrupt a running turn. Delivery survives container restarts. Granularity is one minute — the scheduler polls every minute, so sub-minute precision is not available. This is a thin wrapper over create_trigger (a one-shot scheduled task bound to this session); the returned trigger_id can be passed to delete_trigger to cancel before it fires, and the scheduled task disables itself after firing once. When telling the user what you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_later", "schema": {"description": "Schedule a message to be delivered back into THIS SESSION at a future time. Use it to remind yourself to resume work, check on something, or continue after a delay. If a turn is running in this session when the message is due, the message waits and is normally delivered after that turn ends. It does not interrupt a running turn. Delivery survives container restarts. Granularity is one minute — the scheduler polls every minute, so sub-minute precision is not available. This is a thin wrapper over create_trigger (a one-shot scheduled task bound to this session); the returned trigger_id can be passed to delete_trigger to cancel before it fires, and the scheduled task disables itself after firing once. When telling the user what you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"at": {"description": "The fire time. For a clock time in a known IANA time zone (the user's '9am Monday'), write the local date and time, then the zone name in brackets, with no UTC offset (e.g. 2026-04-20T09:00:00[America/Los_Angeles]). The server works out the offset for that date, so do not work it out yourself. Otherwise write an RFC3339 timestamp (e.g. 2026-04-20T17:00:00Z). Seconds are truncated. Must be in the future. Mutually exclusive with 'delay_minutes' — set exactly one.", "type": "string"}, "delay_minutes": {"description": "Fire this many minutes from now. Minimum 1. Mutually exclusive with 'at' — set exactly one.", "minimum": 1, "type": "integer"}, "initiation": {"description": "Who wanted this message scheduled. Defaults to own_followup (your own check-in on in-flight work); pass human_request when a person asked you to remind them or to come back at a